before:
This example copies both the changed array and object.
```javascript
const before = [{ id: "a", done: false }];
const after = before.map(task => ({ ...task, done: true }));
console.log(before[0].done);
console.log(after[0].done);
```
It prints false, then true. The object spread copies properties into a new object. The later done property replaces only that property in the new copy.

A conditional update can leave most objects shared.
```javascript
const tasks = [{ id: "a", done: false }, { id: "b", done: true }];
const changed = tasks.map(task => task.id === "b"
  ? { ...task, done: !task.done }
  : task);
console.log(changed[1].done);
console.log(changed[0] === tasks[0]);
```
It prints false and true. ! reverses a Boolean. === between objects checks whether they are the very same object.

after:
This example makes a new array and a new task object.
```javascript
const before = [{ id: "a", done: false }];
const after = before.map(task => ({ ...task, done: true }));
console.log(before[0].done);
console.log(after[0].done);
```
Output:
```text
false
true
```
`{ ...task, done: true }` copies the task's properties, then sets `done` in the copy. The original stays false.

This example changes only task `b`.
```javascript
const tasks = [{ id: "a", done: false }, { id: "b", done: true }];
const changed = tasks.map(task => task.id === "b"
  ? { ...task, done: !task.done }
  : task);
console.log(changed[1].done);
console.log(changed[0] === tasks[0]);
```
Output:
```text
false
true
```
`!task.done` reverses true and false. The last line is true because the unchanged first task is still the same object.
